      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. so for the APA one, is it always et al. even the very first time i mention them if there are more than 3? and for chicago, if i'm doing the author-date version, do i still need to list all 5 names in the actual text or just use the first author? i'm trying to make sure i don't make a mess of the bibliography too.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, for APA 7th edition, if a work has three or more authors, you use the first author's last name followed by \"et al.\" for *every* citation, including the very first one. For the author-date Chicago style, you generally follow the same pattern as APA when citing in the text; you use the first author's last name followed by \"et al.\" if there are four or more authors, though this can sometimes vary slightly depending on the specific Chicago manual edition you are following. Always double-check the most current Chicago guide to be certain about the exact threshold for using \"et al.\" in the text.",
